\section{Problem statement and stability objective}
\label{sec:study-purpose}

This paper considers the stability of an adaptive quaternion multiplicative
extended Kalman filter for marine inertial wave-motion estimation. The
estimator uses an attitude observer during initialization and subsequently
operates as a full-state QMEKF. Consequently, the analysis must address both
finite-time entry into the QMEKF operating regime and stability of the
subsequent hybrid estimation dynamics.

The objective is regional practical stability of attitude, gyro-bias, and
wave-motion errors, together with bounded accelerometer-bias error. Practical
stability permits a nonzero ultimate bound induced by bounded sensor error,
model mismatch, and physical bias variation. Absolute heading requires
independent directional information and is treated separately from the
gravity-observable dynamics.

The proof therefore has three steps: establish admissible startup and entry,
prove invariance of the Live operating domain under all admissible updates,
and establish error dissipation over informative observation intervals. The
analysis below uses only the complete finite-word approach developed in this
paper.

\section{Why the admissible motion class is restricted}
\label{sec:study-motion}

An inertial-only estimator cannot recover arbitrary translations, arbitrary
sensor offsets and arbitrary heading from unrestricted sensor sequences.
Constant acceleration integrates into unbounded velocity, an unremoved
constant displacement integrates into the integral state, and gravity alone
does not observe heading. Restricting the theorem to a physical operating
class is therefore substantive, not a device for making a numerical check
pass. Each bound is declared before the proof; a failed proof is not permission
to discard troublesome physical histories.

\subsection{The physical wave coordinate and one history}
Write the vessel reference-point motion as
\begin{equation}
 x(t)=x_{\rm eq}(t)+p_m(t),\qquad
 \dot p_m=v_m,\qquad \dot v_m=a_m .
 \label{eq:study-physical-jet}
\end{equation}
The slowly changing equilibrium contains current, propulsion, leeway and the
choice of global position origin. The quantity estimated here is the
oscillatory displacement $p_m$, not $x$. An acceleration of $x_{\rm eq}$ that
actually reaches the IMU cannot be erased by this notation: it must be
compensated by an implemented model or retained as a declared disturbance.
Attitude, angular velocity and accelerometer specific force must be generated
by that same physical history. One may not select independent per-sample
acceleration vectors, velocities and displacements from their separate bounds.

We define the \emph{bounded recurrent marine-motion} (BRMM) class as a
continuous family of vessel motions with bounded oscillatory displacement,
velocity, acceleration, and angular rate, together with recurrent quiet or
oscillatory observation windows. All kinematic quantities are generated by
one continuous physical history; they are not selected independently at each
sample. The class excludes persistent acceleration bias in the physical motion
and imposes the all-time displacement-primitive condition
\begin{equation}
 \left\|\int_u^t p_m(s)\,ds\right\|\le D_S
 \quad\text{for every }u\le t\text{ on every admitted continuation}.
 \label{eq:study-brmm-primitive}
\end{equation}
This excludes a permanent nonzero wave-displacement DC component without
excluding quiet water or finite intervals of nearly constant displacement.
It is stronger than bounded $p_m,v_m,a_m$, an empirical mean near zero, or a
finite list of accepted recurrence windows. For example $p_m(t)=p_0\ne0$ has
zero $v_m,a_m$ but an unbounded primitive. Even the bounded family
$p_n(t)=\cos(t/n)$ has primitive diameter $2n$; amplitude bounds alone give no
uniform $D_S$.

\subsection{Current padded BRMM envelope}
\label{sec:study-motion-envelope}
The admissible physical envelope is summarized below. These
are theorem-domain limits, not a claim that every vessel or every sea fits
them. They must be checked jointly on a continuous history.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
Physical quantity & Declared upper bound or band\\
\midrule
Significant wave height $H_s$ & $9.35\,\mathrm m$\\
Wave displacement $\|p_m\|$ & $8.10\,\mathrm m$\\
Wave velocity $\|v_m\|$ & $5.50\,\mathrm{m/s}$\\
Wave acceleration $\|a_m\|$ & $8.80\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$\\
Physical angular rate $\|\omega_b\|$ & $35\,\mathrm{deg/s}$\\
Declared spectral-certificate frequency band & $[0.018,0.88]\,\mathrm{Hz}$\\
Displacement-primitive diameter $D_S$ & $1100\,\mathrm{m\,s}$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The padding is declared relative to the reference operating envelope; it is
not fitted to a replay that happened to converge. A significant-height limit
or a spectral plot does not prove the pathwise peak and primitive limits.
The acceleration ceiling lies below $g=9.80665\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$; the resulting
$g-8.8=1.00665\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$ physical specific-force margin is \emph{before}
measurement bias and noise. It does not by itself prove an accepted gravity
gate or a correct Mahony handoff.

A harmonic or spectral realization with a hard inverse-frequency amplitude
moment gives a useful sufficient certificate of
\eqref{eq:study-brmm-primitive}: if $p_m=\dot\phi$ and
$\|\phi\|\le M_{-1}$, then $D_S\le2M_{-1}$. A bounded shaping-state potential
can supply the same argument. These are ways to establish the physical
condition, not a requirement to replace arbitrary admitted motions by one
sinusoid. The detailed original lemmas sometimes use a smaller baseline
budget; their constants do not automatically certify this padded domain.
\studysource{docs/ou3-brmm-main-handover.md}
\studysource{tools/stability/ou3_brmm_complete_source.py}

\subsection{Quiet and oscillatory windows are not full observability}
BRMM separates quiet windows from recurrent oscillatory ones. On a window,
center the acceleration and form its energy. The quiet branch permits small
energy, including exactly zero motion; there is no universal minimum wave
amplitude. The oscillatory branch requires the declared energy and separated,
opposing acceleration-lobe conditions. The impulse/primitive bounds remain
in force on both branches. Their exact constants and quantifiers are given
in Sec.~\ref{sec:brmm-source} through its source
contract; they are not inferred from a plot.

These conditions constrain temporal physical motion. They do not imply a
rank-two heading/gyro-bias information inequality, an all-time accepted
magnetometer stream, or a favorable sampled mean of normalized gravity.
Those are separate assumptions or proof obligations. In particular, a
continuous primitive bound cannot be reused unchanged as a sampled bound
without a sampling-error argument.

\subsection{The integral origin is fixed once}
\label{sec:study-origin}
The analysis uses the physical comparison coordinate
\begin{equation}
 S_L(t)=\int_{t_L}^{t}p_m(s)\,ds,\qquad S_L(t_L)=0,
 \label{eq:study-live-origin}
\end{equation}
where $t_L$ is the actual first Live time. The original detailed entry graph
also uses a session primitive $S_0(t)=\int_0^t p_m(s)\,ds$. They differ by the
one constant $S_0(t_L)$: $S_L=S_0-S_0(t_L)$. Translating a proof between these
conventions requires carrying that constant through its error and supply
coordinates. Neither convention reanchors physical position or resets the
integral at each word, magnetic refinement, or hold/release event. Such
restarting would hide accumulated physical forcing and change the theorem.

\section{Biases, sensor errors, and deployment scope}
\label{sec:study-biases}

\subsection{Why true bias, estimated bias, and bias error differ}
Let $\beta$ denote the entire true residual accelerometer bias in the
\emph{implemented temperature-centered coordinate}, $\hat b_a$ the estimate,
and $e_{ba}=\beta-\hat b_a$. Removing a configured temperature mean does not
remove unknown constant offset, temperature error, hysteresis or PCB strain.
All terms left by the actual calibration remain in $\beta$ or an explicitly
named measurement-model disturbance. Scale-factor error multiplies the
physical signal and cannot silently be treated as a constant offset.

The estimator's bias prior is a model, not a physical decay law. During hold,
the estimated bias and its covariance treatment change, but the true sensor
bias continues to evolve. On a prediction with true and estimated transition
factors $\phi_t,\phi_e$, the composite true-history form gives
\begin{equation}
 \beta^+=\phi_t\beta+w,\qquad
 e_{ba}^{-,+}=\phi_e e_{ba}^{+}+(\phi_t-\phi_e)\beta+w .
 \label{eq:study-bias-prediction}
\end{equation}
The first $+$ on $\beta$ means the next physical endpoint; $e_{ba}^{-,+}$ is
the next prior error. If deterministic offset terms are kept separately,
the same identity includes $d_{{\rm det},k+1}-\phi_e d_{{\rm det},k}$.
An accepted correction adds $-K_b r$ and the actual projection residual to
the error. That innovation and projection depend on the estimator and must
remain inside the dynamics, not become independent exogenous noise.

\subsection{The three mandatory source families}
\label{sec:study-bias-families}
The BIAS0/BIAS1/BIAS2 families represent different kinds of physical history.
The proof must admit each separately, with one persistent root and parameter
history, not substitute whichever family is easiest to certify.
\begin{description}
\item[BIAS0: physical composition.]
A Gauss--Markov component is combined with all unremoved deterministic offset,
temperature, strain and non-GM terms. Its sampled recurrence retains the driver
from that same composite history. Hard increment bounds are pathwise
hypotheses; they are not derived from a noise PSD or standard deviation alone.
\item[BIAS1: one root and one parameter history.]
A representative form is
$\beta(t)=b_0e^{-t/\tau}+A\sin(\omega t+\varphi)+d_{\rm det}(t)$,
componentwise with qualified parameter bounds. The root, decay parameter,
amplitude, frequency and phase persist across samples. The driver bound comes
from the exact difference $\beta(t+h)-\phi_t\beta(t)$, not independently
selected endpoint biases.
\item[BIAS2: bounded non-relaxing drift.]
The physical history has bounded magnitude and variation, and the transition
interval includes $\phi_t=1$, equivalently the infinite correlation-time
limit. A bias is not required to decay merely because the filter has a finite
bias-prior time constant. The same physical drift supplies both true-bias and
bias-error equations.
\end{description}
The bias families impose a true-bias component ceiling
$0.13\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$, hence a norm ceiling $\sqrt3\,0.13\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$.
A parameter token in the executable contracts forces the word composer to
retain one history. A per-sample box that forgets the token is not the declared
family. The source-family validators establish their own algebraic bounds;
they do not alone prove correct attachment to every shipping event.
\studysource{tools/stability/ou3_alt_contraction/bias_families.py}
\studysource{tools/stability/ou3_p4_bias0_family.py}
\studysource{tools/stability/ou3_p4_bias1_family.py}
\studysource{tools/stability/ou3_p4_bias2_family.py}

\paragraph{A naming distinction in the detailed lemmas.}
The older detailed sensor section uses ``BIAS0'' for physical qualification,
``BIAS1'' for true/error transport, and ``BIAS2'' for an \emph{optional
measurement-separation sector}. That last BIAS2 condition is not the
non-relaxing source family above. The sector can strengthen motion gains only
if its own inequality is proved on the relevant measurement graph. It is not
implied by bounded drift, by the wave band, or by naming a source ``BIAS2''.
No bias-convergence or measurement-separation assumption is needed for the
simple compactness implication
\begin{equation}
 \|\hat b_a\|\le R_b,\quad \|\beta\|\le B_b
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \|e_{ba}\|\le R_b+B_b .
 \label{eq:study-bias-compactness}
\end{equation}
Here the configured projection radius is $R_b=0.4\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$; preservation
of its actual machine implementation remains part of runtime qualification.
Compact bias error does not mean small motion error or contraction of the
coupled covariance-weighted state.

\subsection{Commissioned sensor envelopes are conditional}
The startup profiles distinguish the BMI270/AtomS3R from the MPU6886/AtomS3.
Their deterministic residual norm envelopes are respectively
$(\|n_a\|,\|n_g\|)\le(0.30\,\mathrm{m/s^2},0.02\,\mathrm{rad/s})$ and
$(0.50\,\mathrm{m/s^2},0.03\,\mathrm{rad/s})$.
The physical gyro-bias contract also bounds its initial value, subsequent
magnitude and drift; the retained limits are $0.01\,\mathrm{rad/s}$,
$0.02\,\mathrm{rad/s}$ and $10^{-5}\,\mathrm{rad/s^2}$, respectively.
These are commissioned-installation theorem hypotheses, not unconditional
joint guarantees copied from component datasheets.

Startup additionally uses a temporal direction-error contract of the form
$r=m+\dot\xi$ with $\|m\|\le0.10$ and $\|\xi\|\le1.5\,\mathrm s$.
This low-frequency/primitive restriction is distinct from instantaneous sensor
amplitude bounds. The detailed continuous observer lemmas retain their own
constants and seed conditions; a larger commissioned envelope cannot be
inserted into a smaller lemma without rechecking its inequality.

For Live arithmetic, the calibrated, mapped binary32 API packets obey
\begin{equation}
 \max_i|\mathrm{gyro}_{B,i}|\le35\,\mathrm{rad/s},\qquad
 \max_i|\mathrm{accel}_{B,i}|\le160\,\mathrm{m/s^2}.
 \label{eq:study-api-bounds}
\end{equation}
These hardware-scale \emph{packet} limits must not be confused with the much
smaller physical wave-rate limit in Sec.~\ref{sec:study-motion-envelope},
which is in degrees per second. All limits, residuals, rounding cells and
physical continuity must hold together. They control arithmetic domains, not
measurement acceptance or end-to-end convergence.

The present analysis fixes the lever arm to zero and excludes wind-heel
retargeting: the wind-heel angle is zero from construction and
\texttt{update\_wind\_heel()} is never called. The admitted vibration-guard
branch remains dormant and transparent; the proof may not quietly disable a
branch that an admitted source actually activates. Sampling, clocks, missing
or rejected samples and the target floating-point environment also belong to
the contract, not to an unmodeled implementation layer.
