\section{Periodic magnetic service: calls, acceptance, and information}
\label{sec:study-magnetic-service}

A magnetometer can supply heading only through a usable horizontal field and
updates that the filter actually accepts. The commissioned magnetic source
The admitted magnetic-field model assumes a world-field magnitude between 20 and
$75\,\mu\mathrm T$, horizontal magnitude at least $15\,\mu\mathrm T$,
body hard iron at most $5\,\mu\mathrm T$, and deterministic residual at most
$2\,\mu\mathrm T$ per theorem sample. This does not establish the whole
startup heading-accumulation history; a tilt-only error bound cannot substitute
for that history.

\subsection{The attempted-call schedule is not an acceptance theorem}
The measurement schedule requires the first post-Live callback within
40 ms and subsequent attempted-call gaps at most 40 ms. Attempt counters and
the internal bias-unlock clock have their own shipping semantics. In
particular, 250 calls and the strict elapsed-time condition are not the same
as 250 accepted innovations. Under continuing locally finite service through
unbounded physical time, the internal unlock condition has its stated timing
bound; an external hold may nevertheless keep H18 active indefinitely.
No eventual A21 transition is asserted for arbitrary external hold histories.
Rejected updates, a pending first-north acquisition, saturation and magnetic
interference cannot be counted as heading information.

\subsection{The conditional accepted informative-service assumption}
\label{sec:study-mag-assumption}
For the strict full-heading conclusion, define a separate Normal-Live regime
after actual Live entry and heading acquisition. Declare constants
$T_B,L_B,h_B,\alpha_B>0$ with $L_B\ge2T_B$. For \emph{every overlapping}
interval $[s,s+L_B]$ contained in that regime, use only accepted, gauged magnetic
updates and their transported whitened sensitivities
\begin{equation}
 B_i=R_i^{-1/2}H_i\Phi_{s,i}E_s .
 \label{eq:study-magnetic-information}
\end{equation}
Here $R_i,H_i$ are the actually applied measurement quantities,
$\Phi_{s,i}$ retains the preceding full same-history event product, and $E_s$
embeds the heading/axial gyro-bias pair in a fixed orthonormal frame with
coordinates measured in radians and radians per second. Arbitrary rescaling
of that frame cannot be used to manufacture an information floor.

An informative event has $\|B_i[:,0]\|\ge h_B$. The gap between such events,
including the two boundary gaps, is at most $T_B$, and
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{i\in[s,s+L_B]}B_i^{\top}B_i\succeq\alpha_B I_2 .
 \label{eq:study-pair-information}
\end{equation}
Periodicity alone does not imply this rank-two inequality. Multiple heading
observations must distinguish heading from its drift caused by gyro bias.
At finite error, the corresponding nonlinear incremental relation is needed;
a tangent formula is neither the derivative of discontinuous rounding nor a
finite-error certificate.

This is an explicit hypothesis of the conditional service theorem, not a
claim that every BRMM history supplies it. A deployment must either
establish membership or state it as an assumption. Finite-window audits are
useful tests but prove neither infinite recurrence nor a nonvacuous infinite
service class. Quiet water remains physically admissible even when full-heading
service is absent. The ungauged conclusion must then be different, as explained
in Sec.~\ref{sec:study-ungauged}.
